(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-gi-i-ii-quiz-version-7"]={"title":"Gastrointestinal Tract I & II — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 7)","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The ileum is located predominantly in which abdominal region?","choices":["Epigastric region","Right lower quadrant","Left upper quadrant","Right upper quadrant"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ileum lies mostly in the right lower quadrant, ending at the ileocecal junction near the cecum. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Compared with the ileum, the jejunum is characterized by which of the following?","choices":["Larger diameter, thicker walls, and greater vascularity","Thinner walls and a smaller diameter throughout","Presence of prominent Peyer's patches in the wall","Termination at the ileocecal junction and valve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The jejunum has a larger diameter, thicker walls, and is more vascular than the ileum. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Aggregated lymphoid nodules known as Peyer's patches are a distinguishing feature of which small-intestinal segment?","choices":["Jejunum","Duodenum","Ileum","All three segments equally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Peyer's patches are a distinguishing feature of the ileum. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"The large intestine is described as approximately how long?","choices":["1.5 meters (5 feet)","3 meters (10 feet)","1 meter (3.3 feet)","6 meters (20 feet)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The large intestine is approximately 1.5 meters (5 feet) long. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"The large intestine has an approximate diameter of:","choices":["2.5 cm (1 inch)","12 cm (5 inches)","20 cm (8 inches)","6.5 cm (2.5 inches)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The large intestine has an approximate diameter of 6.5 cm (2.5 inches), larger than that of the small intestine. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"The large intestine's main functions include storing feces and absorbing most of the remaining:","choices":["Nutrients and vitamins only","Proteins exclusively","Fats exclusively","Water and electrolytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The large intestine absorbs most of the remaining water and electrolytes from digested material, solidifying it into feces. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"The internal anal sphincter is composed of which tissue type and control?","choices":["Skeletal muscle, involuntary control","Skeletal muscle, voluntary control","Cardiac muscle, involuntary control","Smooth muscle, involuntary control"],"answer":3,"exp":"The internal anal sphincter is smooth muscle and is not under voluntary control. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"The external anal sphincter is composed of which tissue type and control?","choices":["Smooth muscle, involuntary control","Cardiac muscle, involuntary control","Skeletal muscle, under voluntary control","Smooth muscle, voluntary control"],"answer":2,"exp":"The external anal sphincter is a ring of skeletal muscle fibers under voluntary control, encircling the distal anal canal. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"The rectum forms approximately the last how many centimeters of the digestive tract?","choices":["15 cm","5 cm","25 cm","10 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rectum forms the last 15 cm of the digestive tract and serves as an expandable organ for temporary fecal storage. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"The liver is described in the lecture as the largest visceral organ and also the largest:","choices":["Gland","Lymphoid organ","Endocrine organ","Muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The liver is the largest visceral organ and also the largest gland in the body. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which of the following is a stated function of the liver in this lecture?","choices":["Bile secretion, detoxification, and clotting protein production","Production of digestive enzymes and intrinsic factor","Absorption of dietary fats and fat-soluble vitamins","Insulin and glucagon secretion from pancreatic islets"],"answer":0,"exp":"Stated liver functions include bile secretion, detoxification, production of blood clotting proteins, storage of glycogen/vitamins/iron/copper, immune support, and fetal hematopoiesis. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"The liver is completely covered by which fibrous capsule?","choices":["Glisson's capsule","Bowman's capsule","Gerota's renal fascia","Tenon's ocular capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"The liver is completely covered by Glisson's capsule, with an incomplete additional covering by peritoneum. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Of the liver's triple blood supply, the hepatic artery supplies approximately what percentage of total flow to liver tissue?","choices":["75%","50%","5%","15%"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hepatic artery supplies approximately 15% of the blood flow to liver tissue, with the portal vein supplying the majority. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"The hepatic veins, part of the liver's triple blood supply, ultimately drain into which chamber or vessel?","choices":["The left atrium of the heart","The portal vein of the liver","The hepatic artery, an inflow vessel","The right atrium (via the IVC)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hepatic veins carry outflow from the liver to the right atrium via the inferior vena cava. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"Which peritoneal ligament of the liver connects the left lobe to the diaphragm and separates it from the right lobe on the diaphragmatic surface?","choices":["Triangular ligament","Falciform ligament","Coronary ligament","Ligamentum venosum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The falciform ligament separates the left and right lobes of the liver on its diaphragmatic surface and attaches the liver to the diaphragm and anterior abdominal wall. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"The hepatic portal vein is formed by the union of the superior mesenteric vein and the:","choices":["Right gastric vein","Left gastric vein","Splenic vein","Inferior mesenteric vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hepatic portal vein is formed by the union of the superior mesenteric vein and the splenic vein. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"The quadrate lobe of the liver is located between the gallbladder and which structure?","choices":["The inferior vena cava","The ligamentum venosum","The porta hepatis","The ligamentum teres"],"answer":3,"exp":"The quadrate lobe lies between the gallbladder and the ligamentum teres. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Within a liver lobule, the portal triad found at its corners consists of a branch of the portal vein, a branch of the hepatic artery, and a:","choices":["Lymphatic trunk","Bile duct","Central vein","Hepatic vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"The portal triad consists of a branch of the portal vein, a branch of the hepatic artery, and a bile duct. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Blood draining from the hepatic sinusoids of a liver lobule first collects into the:","choices":["Central vein","Portal vein","Bile duct","Hepatic artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Blood from the hepatic sinusoids drains into the central vein at the center of each liver lobule. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"The three named parts of the gallbladder are the fundus, body, and:","choices":["Isthmus","Neck","Infundibulum only","Antrum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gallbladder's three parts are the fundus, body, and neck. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"The gallbladder lies between which two lobes of the liver?","choices":["Right and caudate lobes","Right and quadrate lobes","Left and caudate lobes","Left and quadrate lobes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gallbladder is positioned between the right and quadrate lobes of the liver. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"The regions of the pancreas, from right to left, include the head with its uncinate process, followed by the neck, body, and:","choices":["Isthmus","Fundus","Ampulla","Tail"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pancreas is organized into the head (with uncinate process), neck, body, and tail, from right to left. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 130"},{"q":"The pancreatic islets, the endocrine tissue of the pancreas, make up approximately what fraction of total pancreatic cells?","choices":["1%","10%","50%","25%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pancreatic islets, scattered throughout the gland, comprise only about 1% of pancreatic cells. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 132"},{"q":"With respect to the peritoneum, the pancreas is best classified as:","choices":["Fully intraperitoneal","Retroperitoneal","Suspended by a long mesentery","Located within the greater omentum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pancreas is retroperitoneal, lying behind the peritoneum against the posterior abdominal wall. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 130"},{"q":"The spleen lies anterior to which ribs?","choices":["Ribs 5–7","Ribs 7–9","Ribs 9–11","Ribs 11–12"],"answer":2,"exp":"The spleen lies anterior to ribs 9 through 11. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 136"},{"q":"Within the spleen, the red pulp is functionally associated with which of the following?","choices":["Hormone secretion into systemic circulation","Lymphatic immune surveillance function","Hematopoietic and blood-filtering function","Bile production and secretion into ducts"],"answer":2,"exp":"The red pulp of the spleen is hematopoietic and filters blood, in contrast to the lymphatic white pulp. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 136"},{"q":"The celiac trunk arises from the abdominal aorta at the aortic hiatus, which is formed between the:","choices":["Two leaves of the lesser omentum","Two crura of the diaphragm","Two layers of the mesentery","Two lobes of the liver"],"answer":1,"exp":"The celiac trunk arises from the aorta at the aortic hiatus, formed between the two crura of the diaphragm. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 139"},{"q":"Which artery, a branch of the common hepatic artery, supplies the region just superior to the duodenum before the hepatic artery continues as the proper hepatic artery?","choices":["Left gastric artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Splenic artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The gastroduodenal artery branches from the common hepatic artery before it continues as the proper hepatic artery. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 142"},{"q":"The proper hepatic artery, a continuation of the common hepatic artery, gives rise to which named branches?","choices":["Superior and inferior mesenteric arteries","Right and left hepatic arteries","Renal and suprarenal arteries","Splenic and left gastric arteries"],"answer":1,"exp":"The proper hepatic artery divides into right and left hepatic arteries supplying the corresponding liver lobes. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 142"},{"q":"The inferior vena cava receives the right gonadal and right suprarenal veins directly, while the corresponding left-sided veins drain first into the:","choices":["Splenic vein","Left renal vein","Left common iliac vein","Left hepatic vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"On the left side, the gonadal and suprarenal veins drain into the left renal vein before it joins the IVC, unlike their right-sided counterparts which drain directly into the IVC. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 146"}]};
